{
  "key": "skulle_vs_ville",
  "concept_slug": "skulle_vs_ville",
  "grounding_mode": "fallback_no_wiki",
  "title": "Choosing between skulle and ville",
  "cefr_level": "B1",
  "goal": "Use skulle and ville to express plans, reported future, willingness, and hypothetical meaning.",
  "objectives": [
    {
      "id": "obj_forms",
      "statement": "Recognize skal, vil, skulle, and ville as related modal forms.",
      "bloom_targets": [
        "understand"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obj_plan_report",
      "statement": "Use skulle for a planned or scheduled action seen from the past, including reported future.",
      "bloom_targets": [
        "understand"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obj_willing_want",
      "statement": "Use ville for willingness, wanting, or refusal in the past.",
      "bloom_targets": [
        "understand"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obj_hypothetical",
      "statement": "Choose skulle or ville in common hypothetical sentences based on obligation/advice versus desire/result.",
      "bloom_targets": [
        "understand"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_kind": "section",
      "id": "imported_section_000",
      "role": "orient",
      "objective_ids": [
        "obj_forms"
      ],
      "title": "Two past modal forms, two jobs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "A modal verb changes the meaning of another verb, such as can, must, will, or shall. In Norwegian, "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "skal",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " and "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "vil",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " are present-tense modal forms. Their simple past forms, simple past meaning the tense used for completed or past-time events, are "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "skulle",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " and "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "ville",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "col_langs": [
            "en",
            "no",
            "no"
          ],
          "headers": [
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Present form"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Simple past form"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Core meaning"
              }
            ]
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "shall, going to, scheduled to"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "skal"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "skulle"
                }
              ]
            ],
            [
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "will, want to"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "vil"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "ville"
                }
              ]
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Both simple past forms can sometimes look like English would, but they do not do the same work. "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Skulle",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " often points to a plan, agreement, duty, or future seen from the past. "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Ville",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " often points to wanting, willingness, refusal, or a hypothetical result."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Jeg skal møte Kari klokka seks."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "I am going to meet Kari at six."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Jeg vil gjerne møte Kari."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "I would like to meet Kari."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "element_kind": "section",
      "id": "imported_section_001",
      "role": "model",
      "objective_ids": [
        "obj_plan_report"
      ],
      "title": "Skulle for plans seen from the past",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Skulle",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " is used when a plan, appointment, agreement, or expected future action is viewed from a past point. The verb after it is normally an infinitive, the base form of the verb, such as "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "møte",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " or "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "reise",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " without "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "å",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " after a modal."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Use "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "skulle",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " plus infinitive for a plan, arrangement, or future action as seen from the past."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Vi skulle reise til Bergen, men toget ble kansellert."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "We were going to travel to Bergen, but the train was canceled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hun sa at hun skulle ringe etter jobb."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "She said that she would call after work."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "In reported speech, reported speech meaning repeating what someone said from a later viewpoint, "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "skulle",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " often corresponds to English was going to or would. The key idea is not willingness; it is a planned future from the speaker's earlier point of view."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "element_kind": "section",
      "id": "imported_section_002",
      "role": "contrast",
      "objective_ids": [
        "obj_willing_want"
      ],
      "title": "Ville for wanting and willingness",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Ville",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " is used when the past meaning is wanted to, was willing to, or would not. It describes a person's will, preference, or refusal rather than a schedule."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Use "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "ville",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " plus infinitive when the point is wanting, agreeing, being willing, or refusing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Han ville hjelpe oss med flyttingen."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "He wanted to help us with the move."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Barnet ville ikke legge seg."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "The child would not go to bed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "callout",
          "level": "warning",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "Do not choose "
                },
                {
                  "kind": "foreign_term",
                  "value": "ville",
                  "lang": "no"
                },
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": " just because English has would. Ask what would means: planned future from the past points to "
                },
                {
                  "kind": "foreign_term",
                  "value": "skulle",
                  "lang": "no"
                },
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "; willingness or wanting points to "
                },
                {
                  "kind": "foreign_term",
                  "value": "ville",
                  "lang": "no"
                },
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "element_kind": "section",
      "id": "imported_section_003",
      "role": "contrast",
      "objective_ids": [
        "obj_hypothetical"
      ],
      "title": "Hypothetical skulle and ville",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "A hypothetical sentence talks about an imagined situation rather than a stated fact. In common hypothetical sentences, "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "skulle",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " and "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "ville",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " still keep different meanings."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Skulle",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " often gives advice, duty, or what ought to happen; "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "ville",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " often gives what someone would want or what would happen as a result."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Du skulle ha sagt fra tidligere."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "You should have said something earlier."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Jeg ville kjøpt den hvis den var billigere."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "I would buy it if it were cheaper."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "In advice or mild criticism about the past, "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "skulle ha",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " plus a past participle, a verb form used after have such as "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "sagt",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " or "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "gjort",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ", often means should have. With "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "ville",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ", the sentence usually describes desire or an imagined result."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
